<exercisegroup component="one-semester" xml:id="exercisegroup-diagonalization-further-similarity-props">

<title>Further properties of similar matrices</title>

<introduction>

	<p>
	In each of the following,
	assume that square matrices <m> A, B </m> are similar via transition matrix <m> P </m>,
	with <m> \inv{P} A P = B </m>.
	</p>

</introduction>

<exercise>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Verify that the squares <m> A^2 </m> and <m> B^2 </m> are also similar,
			via the same transition matrix <m> P </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<hint><p><m> B^2 = B B </m>.</p></hint>

	</task>

	<task><p>
		Do you think the same is true for every pair of powers <m> A^k </m> and <m> B^k </m> (with the same positive exponent on both matrices)?
	</p></task>

	<task><p> What about <m> \inv{A} </m> and <m> \inv{B} </m>? </p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<task><p>
		Suppose that <m> \uvec{x} </m> is a vector in the null space of <m> B </m>.
		Verify that the transformed vector <m> P \uvec{x} </m> is in the null space of <m> A </m>.
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		What matrix will transform null space vectors of <m> A </m> into null space vectors of <m> B </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		The first two tasks of this exercise can be extrapolated to conclude that the entire null space of <m> B </m> can be transformed into the null space of <m> A </m>,
		and vice versa.
		Combine this with the patterns of <xref ref="proposition-col-row-null-space-dep-indep-of-images" /> to verify that <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> must have the same
		<xref ref="definition-col-row-null-space-nullity" text="title" />.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Suppose that <m> \uvec{b} </m> is a vector in the column space of <m> B </m>.
			Verify that the transformed vector <m> P \uvec{x} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<hint><p>
			Recall that a vector <m> \uvec{b} </m> is in the column space of <m> B </m> if and only if the system <m> B \uvec{x} = \uvec{b} </m> has at least one solution.
		</p></hint>

	</task>

	<task><p>
		What matrix will transform column space vectors of <m> A </m> into column space vectors of <m> B </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		The first two tasks of this exercise can be extrapolated to conclude that the entire column space of <m> B </m> can be transformed into the null space of <m> A </m>,
		and vice versa.
		Combine this with the patterns of <xref ref="proposition-col-row-null-space-dep-indep-of-images" />,
		along with <xref ref="corollary-col-row-null-space-colspace-basis-dim">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="corollary-col-row-null-space-colspace-basis" />,
		to verify that <m> A </m> and <m> B </m> must have the same rank.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<introduction><p>
		We have seen that similar matrices have the same eigenvalues
		(<xref ref="proposition-diagonalization-similar-matrices-properties-same-evalues">Statement</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-diagonalization-similar-matrices-properties" />).
	</p></introduction>

	<task><p>
		Suppose that <m> \uvec{x} </m> is a vector in the eigenspace for a particular eigenvalue <m> \lambda </m> of <m> B </m>.
		Verify that the transformed vector <m> P \uvec{x} </m> is in the eigenspace of <m> A </m> for that same (shared) eigenvalue <m> \lambda </m>.
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		What matrix will transform eigenvectors of <m> A </m> into eigenvectors of <m> B </m>?
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		The first two tasks of this exercise can be extrapolated to conclude that the entire eigenspace of <m> B </m> for <m> \lambda </m> can be transformed into the eigenspace of <m> A </m> for <m> \lambda </m>,
		and vice versa.
		Combine this with the patterns of <xref ref="proposition-col-row-null-space-dep-indep-of-images" /> to verify that the
		<xref ref="definition-diagonalization-geom-mult" text="title" /> of <m> \lambda </m>
		must be the same for both <m> A </m> and <m> B </m>.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
